## The fix

`real()` now walks up whenever `realpathSync` fails, not only when the path is missing:

```ts
for (;;) {
  const up = dirname(head);
  if (up === head) return path;
  try { return resolve(realpathSync(head), ...tail); }
  catch { tail.unshift(...); head = up; }
}
```

So it resolves the longest prefix that resolves and keeps the rest as written. This is the fallback the function already used for paths that do not exist; it now also covers paths this process may not resolve. A policy must reach a decision for every call — a crash means no `tool_result` at all — and an unresolved path is compared literally, which is the stricter reading: `~/.ssh/id_rsa` still matches the store `~/.ssh`, and every secret-path denial test still passes unchanged. I did not touch the tests, and nothing is skipped or ignored.

Honest limit: I could not construct a portable unit test for "exists but cannot be resolved" (it takes an `EPERM`-style failure, not a missing file or a broken link), so this branch is covered here by the six previously failing tests, which exercise it against the real `~/.ssh` on this machine rather than by a new test.

